---
description: sizeColumnsToFit was called while the grid had no width, usually because it was not visible yet.
---

## Grid Had No Width to Fit

`sizeColumnsToFit()` divides the grid's available width between the columns, so it needs the grid to have a width. This message means the grid body measured zero pixels wide, and the columns were left at their existing widths.

{% note %}
The grid does not give up on the first attempt. It re-measures immediately, then after 100ms, then after 500ms, and only warns once all four attempts return zero. Wrapping your own call in a longer `setTimeout` is therefore unlikely to help.
{% /note %}

A grid measuring zero wide almost always means it is not on screen, or its container has not been given a size.

### Check the container has a size

The grid fills its container, so the container needs a height and width of its own. A container with no explicit size, or one whose parent has collapsed, gives the grid nothing to fill. See [Grid Size](./grid-size/).

### Check the grid is visible when you call

The call often runs while the grid is hidden, leaving nothing to measure:

- In a tab, accordion, modal or drawer that is not the active one. Anything hidden with `display: none` has zero size, so call `sizeColumnsToFit()` when the panel becomes visible rather than when it is created.
- Before the container has been laid out, for example immediately after mounting rather than from a grid event.
- In a collapsed flex or grid child, where the parent has given the column no width.

Note that `visibility: hidden` and `opacity: 0` still occupy space, so those are safe. `display: none` is not.

### Prefer declaring the strategy

For the usual case of fitting the columns when the grid loads, the [`autoSizeStrategy`](./column-sizing/#auto-size-columns-to-fit-grid) grid option suits better than an imperative call, because the grid applies it at the right moment itself:

```{% frameworkTransform=true spaceBetweenProperties=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    autoSizeStrategy: {
        type: 'fitGridWidth',
    },
}
```

To have the columns keep sharing the width as the grid resizes, use [Column Flex](./column-sizing/#column-flex) instead. `flex` is maintained continuously, so it survives the container being hidden and shown again without any call from you.

### If you must call it yourself

Call it once the grid is visible. Reacting to the container becoming visible, through a tab-change handler or a `ResizeObserver`, is more reliable than a delay because it does not depend on how long layout takes.
